.SH NAME
lst \- list items in the trash folder
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IX Header "SYNOPSIS"
.Vb 1
\&    lst [OPTION...] [PATH]
.Ve
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IX Header "DESCRIPTION"
The \fBlst\fR command lists files that have been moved to a trash
location \- i.e. "deleted". The output format is similar to \f(CW\*(C`ls \-g\*(C'\fR
with the deletion time appended to each line, in square brackets.
.PP
.Vb 3
\&    $ lst
\&    [today, /home/user]
\&    \-rw\-r\-\-r\-\-  1 409K May 23  2025 \*(AqMy account info.zip\*(Aq       [16:35]
\&
\&    [yesterday, /home/user]
\&    \-rw\-r\-\-r\-\-  1 411K Apr 16 15:21 \*(AqPeek 2026\-04\-16 15\-21.gif\*(Aq [09:28]
\&    \-rw\-r\-\-r\-\-  1 411K Apr 16 15:19 \*(AqPeek 2026\-04\-16 15\-21.gif\*(Aq [07:19]
\&    drwxrwxr\-x  5 4.0K Dec 11  2025  perl5/                     [07:53]
.Ve
.PP
Output rows are grouped by (deletion date, parent directory) then
sorted by (name ascending, deletion time descending).
.PP
The \f(CW\*(C`PATH\*(C'\fR argument filters the list and defaults to the current
working directory. It may refer to an item that was itself previously
moved to the trash.
.PP
.Vb 3
\&    $ lst deleteddir
\&    [2026\-04\-25, /home/user]
\&    drwxrwxr\-x  5 4.0K Nov 11  2024  deleteddir                 [19:01]
\&
\&    [2026\-01\-11, /home/user/deleteddir]
\&    \-rwxrwxr\-x  5 4.0K Dec 11  2025  my\-file                    [15:42]
.Ve
.PP
Filesystem specific trash folders may exist in addition to the \f(CW$HOME\fR
trash folder (which is typically \f(CW\*(C`$XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash\*(C'\fR). They are
created for performance reasons, to ensure that a "deletion" does not
require an expensive copy operation. This leads to the concept of the
\&\fIcurrent trash folder\fR for any given \f(CW\*(C`PATH\*(C'\fR.
.SH OPTIONS
.IX Header "OPTIONS"
.IP "\-\-all\-items, \-a" 4
.IX Item "--all-items, -a"
List all trash items in the current trash folder. Equivalent to
using \f(CW\*(C`\-\-recursive\*(C'\fR (see below) from a filesystem mount directory.
.IP "\-\-all\-trash, \-A" 4
.IX Item "--all-trash, -A"
List all trash items from all known trash locations. Equivalent to
using \f(CW\*(C`\-\-all\-items\*(C'\fR at the top directory of each filesystem. Note that
this can be expensive as it requires probing and potientially awakening
slow external storage.
.Sp
Raises an error when \f(CW\*(C`PATH\*(C'\fR is given.
.IP "\-\-interactive, \-i" 4
.IX Item "--interactive, -i"
Run in Terminal User Interface (TUI) mode to interactively select and
restore items.
.IP "\-\-trash\-dir, \-T" 4
.IX Item "--trash-dir, -T"
Print the current trash \fIdirectory\fR instead of its contents. Can be
combined with \f(CW\*(C`\-\-all\-trash\*(C'\fR to list all trash locations.
.IP "\-\-recursive, \-R" 4
.IX Item "--recursive, -R"
Recursively list deletions from \f(CW\*(C`PATH\*(C'\fR and all of its subdirectories.
.SH FILES
.IX Header "FILES"
Trash folder information may be read from \fR\f(CI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR\fI/rmwrc\fR or
\&\fI\fR\f(CI$HOME\fR\fI/.config/rmwrc\fR
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IX Header "SEE ALSO"
rmw(1)
.PP
The freedesktop.org Trash Specification <https://specifications.freedesktop.org/trash/latest>.
